A||||16||mrfiero||black, gooey stuff||||8||
Z||000000||mrfiero||08-20-2001||02:32 AM||||OK....I decided that my 16K mile '88 GT needed a subwoofer system.  I had a brand new factory speaker and a near new '88 tan maplight slider as well as a near new EQ radio, so I figured what the heck.  <P>I carefully loosened the B pillar trim panel and removed the A pillar trim to access the wiring harnes that runs up the A pillar to the maplight.  <P>I removed the old harness and ran the new one and began reinstalling everything.  I took my time and everything was going smoothly until the B pillar trim panel.  There is a white plastic push pin that helps hold the panel tight and it caused the panel to slip as I was trying to fit it back into its proper place.  <P>What this did was allow the rear corner (near the rear window) of the B pillar panel to come in contact with the headliner.  As you know, there is some black, gooey stuff that sticks to the panel when it is removed.  So now there is a small 1" line of black crap on the corner of my headliner!  <P>To make a long story short, I need to know what to use to get this black stuff off of my headliner without messing anything up (I don't want to further stain or discolor the headliner).  The interior is perfect, except for this stain.  Granted, you can't see it unless you look through the rear window, but I know it's there.  Any help would be appreciated.<P>Thanks.  ||||reg||8
Z||000001||MrPBody||08-20-2001||07:13 AM||||I have the same black yuck on my headliner, from the same source.  Sorry, I don't have a solution yet.  Probably, solvents would spread the stain.  Maybe I'll paint over it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||08-20-2001||08:23 AM||||While creating my removable rear window I got the suff on my new beechwood t-top headliner too. It was in a spot that would of been visible. I was able to completely clean it off with lacquer thinner. I used a clean rag and kept moving to a clean area of the cloth.  After nearly all was removed I started to flood the area of the headliner with thinner and dab it off till it was clean.  A few days later I somehow got a glob of that darn stuff on my beechwood drivers seat bottom and was able to remove it the same way. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7240006.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||08-20-2001||08:55 AM||||Skitime,<P>Would you mind sharing details of your deck lid window install?  It looks great.<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||08-20-2001||09:09 AM||||Hey Skitime.. I got this nice peace of swampland in Arizona I'll trade ya for that GT.. or like I've offered to DXR_Dad, my soul is still on the table  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/sig.gif">  Ray -    <A HREF="mailto:galen@ezrpg.com">galen@ezrpg.com</A>    - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.FieroLinks.f2s.com</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - ICQ: 98668558||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||08-20-2001||09:36 AM||||I hate to sound like an infomercial, but I've had a lot of good luck with "Quick n'Brite", but I've had better luck with....(see end of message)  It sounds like the black goo your talking about is pretty thick.  I wouldn't try to take it off all at once, but to lightly work at it with lots of patients so not to damage the material.  If the goo is showing up on your cleaning cloth and no damage is showing on the headliner, you probably have a good technique.  Patients grasshopper.  The stronger the cleanser, the more risk you run at ruining the material.  OK, you know this already.  <P>     Now my secret tip.  I've used MANY cleanser through the years with good and horrible luck.  I've had extreme good luck with what else???? Sneaker cleaner from the Foot Locker Shoe store of all places that comes out in a foam that you lightly work in.  It surprized me at what it could do.  What's worse to clean than a dirty old sneaker???  A dirty old Fiero?<P>Gets rid of that athletes fungus between the treads!!!   J/K <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||heavyhorse||08-20-2001||10:05 AM||||I was doing a speaker replacement on my rear speakers and had that black stuff all over the headliner.  WD40 worked like a charm and removed ALL of it.  I simply spayed the WD40 on a paper towel and slowly kept wiping it. It didn't even smell like WD40 when I was done!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||08-20-2001||05:17 PM||||When I was swaping a sunroof into my car I got the black goop one my seats in a few spots. I don't know why they used that cheap stuff, typical cheap GM I guess. Anyway I used wd-40 and it worked great for me also.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mope||08-20-2001||06:14 PM||||brake clean will almost remove ANY stain...but it smells and may melt the foam backing on the headliner material.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||08-20-2001||07:37 PM||||Galen,...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> lol<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||12
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-20-2001||09:45 PM||||skitime,  Can you put up more pics of the backwindow? (and directions?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||goldiron||08-20-2001||10:57 PM||||Had the same problem and used a product called Tuff Stuff from O'Rielly's auto part store to remove that goop.  Its a general purpose vinyl/carpet/fabric cleaner that comes out as a foam. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||RossT||08-21-2001||03:58 AM||||My t-tops had some of that black stuff on the headliner too.  I used "goof-off" on q-tips.  Works really well.  Buy the stuff in the paint dept. at Menards.  Caution, it will discolor plastic...||||reg||1
Z||000013||mbramble||08-21-2001||07:37 AM||||Reading this thread yesterday must have jinxed me when I put my interior back in last night since I ended up with black goop on the rear of my headliner!!  A few days before I was cleaning all the plastic pieces to prepare them for painting and used MarHyde Total Prep to clean the goop off the plastic. It just melted it away (the goop, that is).  So, I put some of it on a clean rag and just brushed it over the gooped area and moved to a clean section of cloth with more Total Prep.  After 4 swipes it was completely gone with no damage to the headliner.||||reg||1
